  "unit": "Unit 12 - Interpreting Linear Models",
  "description": "Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 12 - Interpreting Linear Models for SAT Math preparation.",
  "duration": 45,
  "content": "<ul>\n<li>Slope is the <strong>change in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> per unit change in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></strong>.  </li>\n<li>Interpreted as a <strong>rate</strong>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br />\nA taxi company charges according to the equation:</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>Cost</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> = miles traveled.  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slope = 2.5 → cost increases by <strong>\\$2.50 per mile</strong>.  </li>\n<li>Intercept = 4 → flat starting fee = <strong>\\$4</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li>Intercept is the <strong>value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></strong>.  </li>\n<li>Often represents <strong>initial condition</strong>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br />\nTemperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> = hours after noon.  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intercept = 70 → temperature at noon = 70°F.  </li>\n<li>Slope = -2 → temperature drops <strong>2°F each hour</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n\n<p>You can use the equation to <strong>predict values</strong>.  </p>\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br />\nA company’s revenue is modeled by:</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5000</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>200</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>\n\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> = months.  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>After 6 months:  </li>\n</ul>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5000</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>200</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6200</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>So revenue = \\$6200.  </p>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li>Models may not apply for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Negative values may be meaningless (e.g., negative time, negative price).  </li>\n<li>Always check context.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br />\nIf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> gives height of rocket (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> seconds), then at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></math>:  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>150</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>Negative height is meaningless → model only valid for small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr />\n\n<p>On the SAT:<br />\n- A line of best fit is given.<br />\n- You may be asked to interpret slope/intercept.<br />\n- Or check if a point fits the model.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 5</strong><br />\nScatterplot shows relation between study hours (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>) and test score (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>). Line of best fit:  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Intercept = 50 → baseline score with 0 study = 50.  </li>\n<li>Slope = 5 → each study hour adds 5 points.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li>Identify <strong>units</strong> for slope/intercept.  </li>\n<li>Watch for unrealistic predictions.  </li>\n<li>If a graph/table is shown, pick two points to compute slope.  </li>\n<li>Double-check whether the model describes growth or decline.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n\n<p>1) A cell phone plan costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> = number of minutes. Interpret slope and intercept.  </p>\n<p>2) A car’s value is modeled as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow><mn>000</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1200</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> = years since purchase. What does slope mean?  </p>\n<p>3) A fitness app predicts weight loss with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> = weeks. Find weight after 5 weeks.  </p>\n<p>4) The line of best fit for data is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Interpret slope in context.  </p>\n<p>5) A model predicts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, what is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>? Is this prediction reasonable?  </p>\n<hr />\n\n<p>1) Intercept = 40 → fixed monthly fee \\<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>→</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>\\</mi></mrow></math>0.10.  </p>\n<p>2) Slope = -1200 → car loses \\$1200 value each year.  </p>\n<p>3) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>170</mn></mrow></math>. After 5 weeks, weight = 170 lbs.  </p>\n<p>4) Slope = 4 → each increase of 1 unit in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> raises <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> by 4.  </p>\n<p>5) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>150</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></math>. Prediction meaningless (negative). Model not valid for large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">\n</code></pre>",
